  "text": "Áine, also written Enya, figures as an Irish goddess, heroine, or spirit. Her name carries the sense of brightness or splendor. Irish legend offers several figures who bear it. Historical figures of the name tend to be male. They link to the Limerick and north Kerry district of Munster. Mythic figures of the name are female and stay in that same district. The name reaches even farther, into Ulster. The leading Áines of myth may share one root or descend from one original. Áine of Knockainy is often called a fairy queen. She is probably an older goddess in reduced form. The site sits on a hill beside Lough Gur, a storied lake in east County Limerick. The source breaks off at this point and gives no further detail. ",
  "objective": "To record the several Irish figures named Áine and their legends.",
  "key_results": [
    "Áine's name means brightness or splendor.",
    "Áine of Knockainy was probably a diminished sun goddess connected with the Tuatha Dé Danann.",
    "Her feast was held on Midsummer night or Lughnasa.",
    "The Fitzgeralds claimed sovereignty in western Munster through descent from her son Geróid Iarla.",
    "Áine of the Fenian Cycle was the lover of Fionn mac Cumhaill."
  ],
  "evidence": "Áine of Knockainy, a fairy queen and probable diminished goddess, inhabited a hill near Lough Gur and was connected with the great goddess Anu, who gave her name to the Tuatha Dé Danann.",
